---
// ANIMAL WASTE CLEANUP — `/animal-waste-cleanup-san-jose/`. Rebuilt as the seventh gold-standard
// individual service page (docs/30-WEBSITE-MASTER-SPEC.md §20, §20A, §21), same approach as
// /hoarding-cleanup-san-jose/, /estate-cleanout-san-jose/, /property-cleanouts-san-jose/,
// /deep-cleaning-san-jose/, /move-out-cleaning-san-jose/, /extreme-cleaning-san-jose/ and
// /rodent-dropping-cleanup-san-jose/: copy reconciled from
// docs/ASEPTACLEAN-SERVICE-LANDING-PAGE-SYSTEM.md §40 under §20A.1's order — landing copy → map
// to current route → check active/gated state → check claims, pricing and scope → check current
// copy authority → then implement.
//
//   01 SERVICE HERO             flat navy field, no photograph — see the image note below
//   02 VISIBLE WASTE VS MATERIAL this page's signature educational movement — the waste you can
//                                 see and the material underneath may be two different problems
//   03 WHERE DID IT REACH?      editorial assessment: area, frequency, surface, edges, contents,
//                                 odor, access, extent — how scope gets decided, not seven cards
//   04 CLEANABLE VS DAMAGED     surface cleaning vs material condition; porous materials; carpet
//                                 and flooring boundary; odor as a clue, not a guarantee
//   05 HOW THE CLEANUP IS SCOPED eight-step field sequence + new-condition/new-decision rule +
//                                 the animal/human-waste boundary
//   06 FIT + PRICE               the honest proof gap, qualification, price-driver logic
//   07 QUESTIONS                 seven, the ones that decide the purchase
//   08 CLOSE                     "Animal waste in the property?"
//
// THE SPECIALIST THESIS IS doc 30 §20A.5's, unchanged: **the visible waste and the affected
// material underneath may be different problems.** Every movement reinforces it without
// repeating the sentence — the hero states it, 02 makes the reframe explicit (visible waste vs
// affected material, conditional language, not "your whole home is contaminated"), 03 is how
// that reframe becomes a scoping method, 04 is the cleanable-surface-vs-damaged-material
// distinction the task brief calls the strongest trust section, 05 is the field sequence plus
// the access/contents and animal-vs-human-waste boundary, 06 is the honest evidence gap plus fit
// and price, 07 answers the objections that decide whether this is the right service at all.
//
// WHAT THIS REPLACES. The previous build rendered through the generic `ServicePageLayout` +
// `doc27ServicePages.animalPage` record — the same "one template, swap the noun" pattern
// §20A.5 and §20A.25 Q14 rule out, and the same pattern the six prior rebuilds already replaced.
// `animalPage` ITSELF IS NOT REMOVED — `ServiceDirectory.astro` and `ServiceProblemChooser.astro`
// still read `animalPage.slug` and `animalPage.complianceClause` off that record (the directory's
// `alwaysList` exception and the chooser's `showCondition` block), so the export stays exactly as
// it is; only this page's rendering is replaced. Old record's `gate` field cited "docs/27-COPY-
// CANONICAL.md §21 release inputs... not on record" as the reason this route ships noindex — that
// gate is about SEO PUBLICATION readiness (insurance wording, pest-boundary confirmation,
// disposal procedure, PPE limits, final claims review), not about whether the service is
// operationally available. See the route-state note below.
//
// ROUTE STATE IS UNCHANGED AND DELIBERATELY SO. Same slug, same canonical, still `noindex,
// follow`, still excluded from sitemap.xml.ts. Unlike rodent/pigeon droppings, this route IS
// present in `megaNav` (src/data/site.ts's Specialty Cleaning group, "Animal Waste") and IS an
// `alwaysList` exception in `ServiceDirectory.astro` and `ServiceProblemChooser.astro` — those
// three components already treat it as a real, linked, operationally-available service, which is
// what AGENTS.md §2's 2026-08-26 owner clarification says it is. No route was created, renamed,
// redirected or re-indexed. This file does not flip `noindex`, and this comment does not
// recommend flipping it — see the final report for what would need to clear first (doc 27 §21's
// release inputs, unrelated to this rebuild).
//
// OPERATIONAL SERVICE STATE — NOT INFERRED FROM INDEXATION. Per doc 30 §20A.4 and AGENTS.md §2's
// 2026-08-26 owner clarification, animal-waste cleanup is NOT operationally gated. It is not
// TSWMP-dependent work. The page below describes it as a real, current service — reviewed,
// scoped and performed within Aseptaclean's current lawful and insured scope — at no point
// implying "coming soon," "future service," or "pending launch."
//
// THE DISINFECT/DECONTAMINATION RECONCILIATION — THE MOST IMPORTANT CONFLICT THIS PAGE RESOLVES.
// docs/ASEPTACLEAN-SERVICE-LANDING-PAGE-SYSTEM.md §40 (rank 6) contains a "CLEANING AND
// DISINFECTING ARE NOT THE SAME THING" section and a process step that says "When disinfecting is
// appropriate for the material and scope, products are used according to their label." Doc 21
// §2.2 (rank 3, above the landing-page file) bans `disinfect` "as an outcome claim" outright — no
// permitted-negation carve-out covers it the way §2.3 carves out the animal/organic clause. That
// section and that process-step sentence are DROPPED from this page in full; they appear nowhere.
// The task brief independently rules out "we sanitize everything," "hospital-grade... decontam-
// ination," and any disinfectant/product-method claim not in current approved operating
// documentation, which the landing-page source's enzyme-cleaner FAQ answer also fails on close
// reading (it names "enzyme cleaners" as a category Aseptaclean selects among) — that FAQ entry is
// also dropped rather than kept in its hedged form, because naming a product category at all is a
// method claim this page has no approved documentation for. Recorded in docs/05-DECISIONS-LOG.md.
//
// DOC 21 §2.3's ANIMAL/ORGANIC LIMITING CLAUSE — verbatim, mandatory wherever animal or organic
// condition work is described. Animal waste cleanup is exactly that, so the clause is required
// here the same way it is on Rodent Droppings, Extreme Cleaning and Specialty Cleaning. Appears
// in movement 04 immediately after the porous-materials boundary. `legal.scopeDisclaimer` appears
// in movement 05, same as the six prior rebuilds.
//
// SPCB / PEST BOUNDARY — ADAPTED, NOT COPIED FROM RODENT. Doc 21 §3's structural-pest-control
// exposure is written around rodents and pigeons specifically (Cal. B&P §8505's express
// inclusion, §8555(g)'s express exclusion of mice/rats/pigeons). This page is domestic pet waste
// — dog and cat urine/feces — which is not itself structural pest control, so the rodent page's
// "Pest Control vs Cleanup" movement is not reproduced wholesale. What IS carried over: this page
// never claims to identify, exclude, trap, or treat any pest or wild animal, and "Wildlife" is
// deliberately NOT offered as an animal-type option (§40's source FAQ intake form listed it; this
// page routes wildlife-caused conditions to a phone conversation rather than presenting it as a
// selectable, pre-scoped path — see movement 03's access note). If a job turns out to involve a
// rodent or a still-active pest presence, movement 05's routing note sends it to Rodent Droppings
// Cleanup or a licensed pest operator rather than absorbing it here.
//
// HUMAN-WASTE / TSWMP BOUNDARY — EXPLICIT, PER THE TASK BRIEF'S STRONGEST WARNING. This page
// never says "urine or feces of any kind" or otherwise generalizes beyond non-human animal waste.
// Movement 05 states directly that human waste, blood, and human biological material are outside
// this service and route to the TSWMP-gated boundary doc 21 §5 sets — no automatic acceptance is
// implied if one is discovered mid-job. Doc 30 §20A.4's three-part separation (operational
// availability / TSWMP gate / SEO state) governs animal waste; TSWMP gate is untouched by this
// page and nothing here narrows or widens it.
//
// CARPET / FLOORING / SUBFLOOR — RESTRAINED, PER THE TASK BRIEF. §40's own approved language
// already declines to promise carpet/pad restoration or flooring replacement ("Do you replace
// carpet or padding? No.") and this page preserves that restraint rather than expanding it. No
// extraction, subfloor treatment, or guaranteed-odor-removal claim appears. Flooring/subfloor
// questions route to "another qualified provider," matching Rodent Droppings' and Extreme
// Cleaning's identical restraint on structural materials.
//
// PRICING — AGENTS.md §4 IS ABSOLUTE. No price figure appears. Cost drivers replace a number,
// same seven-prior-page pattern. No odor-treatment "packages" or tiers — the task brief bars
// them explicitly and nothing in current pricing data supports them.
//
// NO CredentialBar ON THIS ROUTE. Same reasoning as the six prior rebuilds — both gated
// credential-bar claims are release-gated by AGENTS.md §3 pending a current COI match.
//
// MOBILE STICKY CTA — TWO ACTIONS, PER §20A.10. MobileCTA.astro's `isTwoAction` flag now
// includes this route (ninth to opt in), rendering "Call | {primaryCta}" instead of the
// three-action default. Same reasoning as the eight prior opt-ins — the visible primary label is
// a single owner-approved string in site data (AGENTS.md §3), not a route-specific relabel.
//
// NO EMBEDDED FORM. CTAs link to /request-assessment/?service=animal-waste, which
// AssessmentForm.astro now recognizes (added alongside this page) and maps to the "Other"
// `property_situation` option — same reasoning as rodent-droppings: animal waste is a condition,
// not a transition, and none of the existing options describe it truthfully.
//
// IMAGES — SAME HONEST PROOF GAP AS RODENT DROPPINGS, EXTREME CLEANUP AND DETAILED DEEP CLEANING.
// docs/06-ASSET-MANIFEST.md §4's atmosphere-image cap (three) is already knowingly exceeded by
// one, with an explicit "do not add a fifth without a new owner decision," and §6 permanently
// prohibits "hazmat or PPE theatre" and "any image implying a regulated service." The task brief
// independently rules out cute-pet stock, sad-pet imagery, cartoon smell lines, fake before/after
// and AI-generated pet mess. No real Aseptaclean animal-waste-cleanup photography exists yet.
// Rather than force a mismatched or risky asset into the role, this page ships with no
// photograph, the same precedent the three pages above already set. Movement 06 states the gap in
// the page's own voice. Replace with real property-condition photography (wide room shot +
// affected-surface detail, same-area before/during/after) once it exists.
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { legal, site } from "@data/site";

const canonicalPath = "/animal-waste-cleanup-san-jose/";

const title = "Animal Waste Cleanup in San Jose | Aseptaclean";
const description =
  "Dog or cat urine and feces affecting a home or property in San Jose or the South Bay & Peninsula? Send photos and we will review the affected area and surfaces before setting a cleanup scope.";

// §20A.8's destination rule + §20A.9's service branch. `?service=animal-waste` is read by
// AssessmentForm.astro (see that component's service-context block, added alongside this page).
const assessmentHref = "/request-assessment/?service=animal-waste";
const phoneHref = site.business.phoneUri;
const smsHref = site.business.smsUri;

// §40's "WHAT ARE YOU DEALING WITH?" categories, reframed as condition recognition rather than a
// four-card grid — the specifics that tell a visitor this page is talking about their property.
const recognition = [
  "repeated urine on a floor, carpet, or baseboard",
  "feces across a room, closet, or storage area",
  "accidents that keep happening in the same spot",
  "a property left with waste after a tenant, pet, or long-term neglect",
  "waste mixed with general clutter or buildup",
  "a normal house cleaner has turned down the job"
] as const;

// §20A.6's editorial field-assessment composition — the factors that determine scope, not eight
// equal SaaS cards. Combines the brief's AREA / FREQUENCY / SURFACE / EDGE CONDITIONS / CONTENTS
// / ODOR LOCATION / ACCESS / EXTENT variables into one composition.
const scopeFactors = [
  {
    label: "Area",
    detail: "Where is the visible waste — one spot, one room, or more than one area?"
  },
  {
    label: "Frequency",
    detail: "Is this one event, or has the same spot been used repeatedly?"
  },
  {
    label: "Surface",
    detail: "Hard and sealed, or a porous material like carpet, wood, or drywall?"
  },
  {
    label: "Edge conditions",
    detail: "Did waste reach baseboards, seams, wall edges, or cabinet toe-kicks?"
  },
  {
    label: "Contents",
    detail: "Is furniture or stored material covering the affected area?"
  },
  {
    label: "Odor",
    detail: "Does a smell remain after visible waste is removed?"
  },
  {
    label: "Access",
    detail: "Can the affected area actually be reached right now?"
  },
  {
    label: "Extent",
    detail: "Localized to one area, or spread across the property?"
  }
] as const;

// Hard, cleanable surfaces vs porous/absorbent materials — §40's "HARD SURFACE OR SOAKED
// MATERIAL?" pairing, kept as the page's central distinction.
const hardSurfaces = ["Tile", "Sealed counters", "Metal", "Glass", "Some sealed floors"] as const;

const porousMaterials = [
  "Carpet and carpet padding",
  "Upholstery",
  "Unfinished wood",
  "Grout",
  "Drywall",
  "Concrete"
] as const;

// §20A.14's field-procedure sequence, built from §40's "WHAT DOES CLEANUP LOOK LIKE?" six steps
// plus the brief's "stop if a new condition changes the scope" and "review the completed work"
// steps, which §40 states elsewhere on the page but not inside its own numbered sequence.
const process = [
  {
    title: "Show us the affected area",
    detail:
      "Send photos — a wide shot of the room, closer photos of the waste, nearby surfaces, and anything blocking access. Tell us what animal was involved and how long this has been happening."
  },
  {
    title: "We identify the surface and material",
    detail: "Tile, wood, carpet, concrete, cabinets, walls — what was affected changes what happens next."
  },
  {
    title: "We determine how much area is involved",
    detail: "One spot, one room, or several areas — the scope follows the condition, not a guess from one photo."
  },
  {
    title: "We define what can be cleaned",
    detail: "Before work starts, you know exactly which surfaces and areas are included."
  },
  {
    title: "We tell you if something needs another solution",
    detail: "If a material looks damaged rather than dirty, we say so rather than clean over it."
  },
  {
    title: "We remove waste and clean the approved surfaces",
    detail: "Solid waste and included material are removed, then approved surfaces are cleaned for their type and condition."
  },
  {
    title: "We stop if a new condition changes the scope",
    detail: "If something changes once work is underway — a material turns out to be more affected than it looked, or a new condition appears — we stop and tell you before going further."
  },
  {
    title: "We review the completed work",
    detail: "When the job is done, we review the cleaned area against the agreed scope."
  }
] as const;

// Doc 21 §2.3's animal/organic limiting clause — verbatim, mandatory wherever animal or organic
// condition work is described. Word-for-word from the source; do not paraphrase.
const complianceClause =
  "Cleaning only — not a decontamination, sterilization, or health-safety determination.";

// §40's "WHAT WE DO NOT HANDLE ON THIS PAGE" list, trimmed to what this page needs to state and
// reconciled against doc 21 — no pest-identification or trapping claim implied by omission.
const notHandled = [
  "Human waste or human biological material",
  "Live animal handling, trapping, or pest control",
  "Flooring, subfloor, or drywall removal",
  "Insulation removal or repair",
  "Structural repair or demolition"
] as const;

const goodFit = [
  "waste affects more than one small, open spot",
  "accidents have happened repeatedly in the same area",
  "you are not sure whether the material underneath was affected",
  "a normal house cleaner has turned down the job",
  "waste is mixed with clutter or general buildup",
  "you want the scope defined, including what may not be cleanable, before work starts"
] as const;

const poorFit = [
  "one fresh, small accident on an easy-to-clean surface",
  "routine litter-box or yard pickup",
  "the main need is carpet replacement or flooring repair",
  "the main need is pest control, trapping, or exclusion"
] as const;

const priceDrivers = [
  "size of the affected area",
  "how many rooms or surfaces are involved",
  "surface and material type",
  "how long the condition has been present",
  "amount of waste and material to be removed",
  "access and items in the way"
] as const;

// §20A.18: 4–7 highest-value, service-specific questions. Reconciled from §40's FAQ set with the
// disinfect/enzyme-product sentence dropped per the top-of-file reconciliation note.
const faq = [
  [
    "Can you remove the smell?",
    [
      "We clean the odor source when it is inside the approved scope. We do not promise that every odor can be removed through surface cleaning — if urine has reached padding, wood, drywall, or another absorbent material, deeper work may be needed, and we will tell you if that is what we are seeing."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "What if urine went through the carpet?",
    [
      "Urine can move through carpet into the padding and material below it. That is often a different job from a surface-level spot, and it is why we look at the material, not just the visible stain."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Do you replace carpet or flooring?",
    ["No. We do not perform flooring or subfloor removal or replacement. If a material looks damaged rather than cleanable, we tell you and the next step may need a different provider."]
  ],
  [
    "What if the waste reached the baseboard or wall edge?",
    ["Edge conditions matter — waste that reached a seam, baseboard, or wall edge can be a different job from waste that stayed on an open surface. Photos of the edge help us tell early."]
  ],
  [
    "What if multiple rooms or the whole property is affected?",
    ["Send photos of each area. A property with waste in several rooms is scoped differently from one localized spot, and we will tell you what that means for the job."]
  ],
  [
    "What if the room is full of belongings?",
    [
      "If furniture or stored items are blocking an affected area, access may have to be solved first. Depending on the property, that can fit under property cleanouts or hoarding cleanup — we will tell you which makes sense."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Can you tell from photos whether the floor needs replacement?",
    [
      "Sometimes photos are enough to see clear damage. Often we cannot confirm what is happening beneath a finished surface without a closer look, and we will say so rather than guess."
    ]
  ]
] as const;

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "Service",
    name: "Animal Waste Cleanup in San Jose",
    serviceType: "Animal waste affected-area cleanup",
    provider: { "@id": new URL("/#business", site.urls.site).href },
    areaServed: { "@type": "City", name: "San Jose" }
  },
  {
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: site.urls.home },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: 2,
        name: "Animal Waste Cleanup",
        item: new URL(canonicalPath, site.urls.site).href
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faq.map(([question, answer]) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: question,
      acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: answer.join("\n\n") }
    }))
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath={canonicalPath} noindex={true} schema={schema}>
  {/* ══ 01 · SERVICE HERO ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      §20A.7: location context → unmistakable service H1 → specialist thesis → service CTA →
      phone as secondary. NO IMAGE — see the top-of-file note on the honest proof gap. */}
  <section class="aw-hero" aria-labelledby="aw-title">
    <div class="aw-hero__shell">
      <div class="aw-hero__content">
        <p class="aw-hero__eyebrow">
          Animal Waste Cleanup · San Jose · {site.location.serviceArea}
        </p>
        <h1 id="aw-title" class="ac-type-h1-hub">Animal Waste Cleanup</h1>
        <p class="aw-hero__lede">
          Pet urine and feces can be a surface-cleaning problem, or they can affect the material
          underneath. We review what was affected before setting the cleanup scope.
        </p>
        <p class="aw-hero__thesis">
          The waste you can see and the material underneath it may be two different problems.
        </p>

        <div class="aw-hero__actions">
          <a class="aw-btn aw-btn--light" href={assessmentHref}>Send Photos of the Area</a>
          {/* Suppression rule (AGENTS.md §3): never render an empty phone link. */}
          {phoneHref && <a class="aw-hero__call" href={phoneHref}>Call {site.business.phone}</a>}
        </div>
        <p class="aw-hero__note">
          Dog or cat waste, repeated accidents, or long-term buildup — send photos first and we
          will tell you the right next step.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 02 · VISIBLE WASTE VS AFFECTED MATERIAL ═════════════════════════════════════════════
      This page's signature movement. §40's "THE MESS YOU SEE MAY NOT BE THE WHOLE PROBLEM"
      reframe, combined with condition recognition per §20A.6 — one composition, not a nine-card
      grid. Conditional language throughout: this is not "if you see a stain, your home is
      contaminated." */}
  <section class="aw-reframe" aria-labelledby="reframe-title">
    <div class="aw-reframe__inner">
      <p class="aw-label">Start here</p>
      <h2 id="reframe-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">
        What you can see is only the first question.
      </h2>
      <p class="aw-lede">
        A visible accident may sit on sealed hard flooring, tile, wood, laminate, carpet, a
        baseboard, or a cabinet edge. Animal waste does not always stay on top of what it lands
        on — depending on the surface and how long it has been there, it may reach the material
        underneath.
      </p>

      <ul class="aw-check-list aw-check-list--grid">
        {recognition.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>

      <p class="aw-statement">
        A single recent accident on a sealed hard surface can be a very different job from
        repeated accidents in the same spot, waste that reached a baseboard or seam, or a room
        where the same area has been used again and again.
      </p>

      <div class="aw-question">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">The useful question</h3>
        <p>
          “There is a stain on the floor” is a starting point, not an answer. The question that
          actually sets the scope is:
          <span class="aw-question__strong">Did the waste stay on the surface, or did it reach into the material?</span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 03 · WHERE DID IT REACH? ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      §20A.6's editorial field-assessment composition — the factors that determine scope, not
      eight equal SaaS cards. */}
  <section class="aw-scope" aria-labelledby="scope-title">
    <div class="aw-scope__inner">
      <p class="aw-scope__label">How scope gets decided</p>
      <h2 id="scope-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">Where did it reach?</h2>
      <p class="aw-scope__lede">
        We do not price a job from one photo of a stain. Before anything is cleaned, we work
        through the same factors on every job:
      </p>

      <dl class="aw-factors">
        {scopeFactors.map((factor) => (
          <div class="aw-factors__row">
            <dt>{factor.label}</dt>
            <dd>{factor.detail}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>

      <div class="aw-photos">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Photos help. You do not need to disturb anything first.</h3>
        <p>
          Useful photos: a wide shot of the affected room, closer photos of the waste, the
          flooring or surface, nearby wall and baseboard edges, and any other affected rooms. You
          do not need to move furniture, pull up flooring, or touch the material just to
          photograph it — send what you can see.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 04 · CLEANABLE SURFACE VS DAMAGED MATERIAL ══════════════════════════════════════════
      One of the strongest trust sections per the task brief. Hard/cleanable vs porous/absorbent,
      odor as a clue rather than a guarantee, and the doc 21 §2.3 clause. */}
  <section class="aw-material" aria-labelledby="material-title">
    <div class="aw-material__inner">
      <p class="aw-label">Cleanable vs damaged</p>
      <h2 id="material-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">Cleaning cannot fix every material.</h2>
      <p class="aw-lede">
        Cleaning may be appropriate for certain accessible, cleanable surfaces. A cleanable
        surface and a damaged material are not the same problem, and we will tell you which one
        we are looking at.
      </p>

      <div class="aw-material__pair">
        <div class="aw-note">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Hard, cleanable surfaces</h3>
          <p>Depending on condition, we may be able to clean affected:</p>
          <ul class="aw-check-list">
            {hardSurfaces.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="aw-note aw-note--stop">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Porous or absorbent materials</h3>
          <p>Waste may soak deeper into:</p>
          <ul class="aw-minus-list">
            {porousMaterials.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
          <p>
            Cleaning the top does not always fix what is underneath. If the material itself has
            been deeply affected — stained, swollen, delaminated, or otherwise damaged — cleanup
            alone may not be enough, and further assessment, specialty treatment, or replacement
            by another provider may be the right next step. We will tell you when we see that.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="aw-odor">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-feature">Odor can be a clue that the affected material extends beyond what is visible.</h3>
        <p>
          But odor can have more than one cause. We do not promise that every odor can be removed,
          and we do not promise a specific method before we know what surface and condition we are
          working with.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="aw-clause">{complianceClause}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 05 · HOW THE CLEANUP IS SCOPED ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      §20A.14: vertical numbered sequence, not eight cards. The access/contents boundary, the
      new-condition rule, and the animal-vs-human-waste boundary — this page's most important
      guardrail per the task brief. */}
  <section class="aw-plan" aria-labelledby="plan-title">
    <div class="aw-plan__inner">
      <p class="aw-plan__label">How the cleanup is scoped</p>
      <h2 id="plan-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">The exact steps depend on the property.</h2>
      <p class="aw-plan__lede">
        A small bathroom with a hard tile floor is not the same job as several rooms, carpet,
        heavy waste, or waste mixed with belongings. The work is built around the condition we
        find.
      </p>

      <ol class="aw-steps">
